# Polaris backup and retention schedule — example crontab.
#
# Install on the host that owns backups and retention:
#
#   sudo install -m 0644 -o root -g root \
#     /opt/polaris/infra/backups/crontab.example /etc/cron.d/polaris
#
# `/etc/cron.d` entries carry a user field (the sixth column) — that is the
# difference from a `crontab -e` file, and pasting one into the other is the
# usual way these end up silently not running.
#
# Every job writes to its own log. Cron mails stdout to the crontab owner by
# default, which on an unattended host means the output goes nowhere; the
# redirects make the record land somewhere an operator can read it. Rotate
# these with logrotate like any other service log.
#
# Environment
# -----------
# Cron runs with a nearly empty environment: no PATH beyond /usr/bin:/bin, no
# shell profile, no `.env`. Every job below therefore sources a file the
# operator owns. Put connection settings there, not in this file — this one is
# checked into the repository.
#
#   /etc/polaris/retention.env   POLARIS_DATABASE_URL, PGHOST/PGUSER/PGPASSFILE,
#                                POLARIS_CLI, POLARIS_BACKUP_DIR, ...
#
# Times are deliberately staggered. The PostgreSQL dump and the ClickHouse
# backup both touch storage hard, and the prune takes a row lock on chains
# that the dump would otherwise wait behind.

SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
MAILTO=""

# --- Backups ---------------------------------------------------------------

# PostgreSQL control-plane snapshot. Daily at 02:10.
10 2 * * *  polaris  set -a; . /etc/polaris/retention.env; set +a; /opt/polaris/infra/backups/pg-dump.sh >> /var/log/polaris/pg-dump.log 2>&1

# ClickHouse analytical tables. Daily at 02:40, after the Postgres dump has
# finished rather than alongside it.
40 2 * * *  polaris  set -a; . /etc/polaris/retention.env; set +a; /opt/polaris/infra/backups/clickhouse-backup.sh >> /var/log/polaris/clickhouse-backup.log 2>&1

# --- Retention -------------------------------------------------------------

# Attribution touchpoint chains idle beyond the processor's attribution
# window. Daily at 03:17.
#
# Daily is ample: the window is 90 days, so a row's eligibility does not
# change quickly, and the delete is a bounded index scan.
#
# This one exits non-zero on a refusal — an unprunable version, or an --idle
# shorter than the version's window. That is deliberate: a retention job that
# silently succeeds while pruning nothing is worse than one that pages. Do not
# add `|| true`.
#
# Scoped per environment on purpose. An UNSCOPED prune (no
# POLARIS_PRUNE_ENV) means every environment including production, so the
# CLI treats it as a production mutation and refuses it without an
# operator token. Splitting the entries keeps the credential on the one
# job that needs it, instead of handing a production token to a job that
# only ever touches development.
17 3 * * *  polaris  set -a; . /etc/polaris/retention.env; POLARIS_PRUNE_ENV=development; set +a; /opt/polaris/infra/backups/prune-attribution-chains.sh >> /var/log/polaris/prune-chains.log 2>&1
22 3 * * *  polaris  set -a; . /etc/polaris/retention.env; POLARIS_PRUNE_ENV=staging;     set +a; /opt/polaris/infra/backups/prune-attribution-chains.sh >> /var/log/polaris/prune-chains.log 2>&1

# Production carries POLARIS_OPERATOR_TOKEN, which is why it sources a
# second, tighter file. Keep that one root-owned and 0600 — it holds a
# credential that can delete rows.
#
# No token provisioned yet? Comment this line out and install the file
# anyway. The two entries above need nothing but a host, the CLI and
# database reachability, so they start working immediately; uncommenting
# this one later is the whole of the remaining change.
27 3 * * *  polaris  set -a; . /etc/polaris/retention.env; . /etc/polaris/retention-production.env; POLARIS_PRUNE_ENV=production; set +a; /opt/polaris/infra/backups/prune-attribution-chains.sh >> /var/log/polaris/prune-chains.log 2>&1

# --- Reference data --------------------------------------------------------

# MaxMind GeoLite2 database for the enrichment stage. Wednesdays at 04:05.
#
# MaxMind publishes GeoLite2 twice weekly; once a week is ample for
# city-level geo and keeps a 60 MB download off the nightly backup
# window. The script verifies the archive and swaps it in atomically, so
# a failed fetch leaves yesterday's database in place rather than
# breaking geo — see infra/geoip/refresh-geoip.sh.
#
# This does NOT affect running processes: the enrichment stage loads the
# database once at boot and stamps that snapshot's identity onto every
# event it enriches, which is what makes "which database produced this
# row" answerable. A rolling restart picks up a new one, and there is no
# urgency — city boundaries are not a fast-moving input.
#
# Needs POLARIS_GEOIP_LICENSE_KEY, so keep that env file root-owned and
# 0600 like the production retention one above.
5 4 * * 3   polaris  set -a; . /etc/polaris/geoip.env; set +a; /opt/polaris/infra/geoip/refresh-geoip.sh >> /var/log/polaris/refresh-geoip.log 2>&1

# --- Not scheduled here ----------------------------------------------------
#
# `pg-restore.sh` is a drill and an incident tool. It refuses to restore over
# an active primary, and scheduling a restore is not a thing anyone wants to
# do by accident.
#
# Audit retention (2 years) has no purge job yet — see
# docs/operations/backup-and-retention.md "Audit retention policy". When one
# lands it belongs here.
